{
  "reviewed_items": [
    {
      "property_id": "PROP-57888860-post-001",
      "review_verdict": "DISPUTED_FP",
      "original_classification": "vulnerability",
      "adjusted_severity": "Informational",
      "reviewer_notes": "Phase 03 correctly identifies that recover_cells (recovery.c:347-350) computes FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_EXT_BLOB coefficients without validating the upper half equals zero, unlike the encoding assertion (eip7594.c:94-96). However, this is not exploitable in the current system. Before recover_cells_and_kzg_proofs is called, the P2P layer must verify cells using verify_cell_kzg_proof_batch against the blob's KZG commitment. The commitment was created from a polynomial with degree < FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_BLOB. Malicious cells that reconstruct to degree >= FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_BLOB cannot satisfy KZG verification against that commitment and will be rejected before recovery occurs. The cryptographic layer (KZG proofs) enforces the degree constraint, making the missing assertion redundant for security. This is a spec-implementation documentation mismatch, not an exploitable vulnerability. 01e requirement (outputs/01e_PARTIAL_W3B4_1771748827.json line 168): 'Recovered polynomial must have degree < FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_BLOB (truncation correctness)' — the spec requirement is met by the KZG verification layer, not by an explicit check in recovery code. By-design trust boundary: untrusted P2P cells are validated cryptographically before recovery.",
      "spec_reference": "01e PROP-57888860-post-001: 'forall recover(cell_indices, cells) -> poly: deg(poly) < FIELD_ELEMENTS_PER_BLOB'. Spec requirement is enforced by KZG proof verification, not by explicit degree check."
    }
